{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filename": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "filepath": "data\\documents\\pdf\\Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology-acne-guidelines-2025.pdf", "page": 4, "title": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology pre-referral guidelines \u2013 acne", "year": "2025", "producer": "Adobe PDF Library 21.5.90", "creator": "Acrobat PDFMaker 21 for Word", "creationdate": "2025-05-20T10:37:28+08:00", "author": "Fiona Stanley Hospital Dermatology", "company": "WA Health", "contenttypeid": "0x010100D20558782E22444F8E6476743C0A7A47", "keywords": "Fiona Stanley Hospital, FSH, dermatology, pre-referral guidelines, acne", "moddate": "2025-05-20T10:37:29+08:00", "sourcemodified": "D:20250520023710", "subject": "Inofrmation on referring, treatment, introduction to acne", "_dlc_dociditemguid": "400bd398-572f-4117-a0d8-763933fbd180", "total_pages": 6, "page_label": "5"}, "page_content": "Dermatology pre-referral guidelines - acne  May 2025 Page 5 of 6 \nOther treatments may be used at the hospital \n\u2022 Typically isotretinoin will be prescribed, in combination with other treatments. \n\u2022 In females, spironolactone may also be considered.  \nWhen to refer \n\u2022 Failed response to conventional therapy (topical and/or oral). Please note a 3-6 month trial \nof oral agents (antibiotics, OCP) is needed to reach maximal efficacy. \n\u2022 Severe inflammatory nodulocystic acne at risk of, or causing, scarring. \n\u2022 Associated significant psychosocial impact. \nEssential information to include in your referral \n\u2022 Treatments patient has trialled. \n\u2022 Severe scarring acne. \n\u2022 Strong family history of scarring acne. \n\u2022 Ongoing signs or symptoms which remain problematic. \nReferences \n1. Paller and Mancini \u2013 Hurwitz Clinical Pediatric Dermatology: A Textbook of Skin Disorders \nof Childhood and Adolescence (6th edution). Elsevier, 2022. \n2. Therapeutic Guidelines (2022). Therapeutic Guidelines- Acne. (Accessed: April 16, 2025). \n3. UpToDate (2025) Acne-pathogenesis, clinical manifestations, and diagnosis of acne \nvulgaris, Acne. (Accessed: April 16, 2025). \nUseful patient resources", "type": "Document"}}